      <h1>Yutori (ゆとり)</h1>
      <p class="concept-hero-subtitle">Leave room to notice, respond and adjust.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="news-meta">October 12, 2026</p>

    <p>
      “Slow down” does not always mean performing exactly the same movement at a
      lower speed. Sometimes it means leaving a moment in which we can notice a
      mistake and do something about it. That can be the difference between finishing
      a repetition and learning from one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Imagine a pair practising an agreed form of ikkyo. Nage, the person performing
      the technique, loses the required position and tries to compensate with faster
      hand movements. Uke, the partner who attacks and receives the technique,
      begins to hurry in response. Both want to complete the task. Neither has
      time left to see where it went off course.
    </p>
    <p>
      The instructor stops the exercise. The pair agrees on a slower pace and returns
      to the position they were shown. On the next attempt, there is time to make
      a small adjustment before the final control. They have not learned a new
      technique. They have changed the conditions for learning. That room to work
      and adjust is what I want to explore through <strong>yutori</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Leeway, not the Japanese word for an elbow</h2>
    <p>
      The Japanese dictionary <em>Digital Daijisen</em> defines ゆとり as having
      spare capacity rather than being cramped. Its examples concern finances
      and mental room. <em>Nihon Kokugo Daijiten</em> also explicitly includes
      mental, not merely physical, space.<sup><a href="#yutori-source-1">[1]</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      Jisho gives English equivalents including <em>leeway</em>, <em>margin</em>,
      <em>reserve</em>, <em>elbowroom</em> and <em>time to spare</em>.
      Elbowroom is a useful image: enough room to move. It is not a literal
      account of the Japanese word's components or its etymology.
      In this article, I use “margin” and “room to adjust”.<sup><a href="#yutori-source-2">[2]</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      You may also encounter the word in <em>yutori kyōiku</em>, Japanese education
      reforms associated with reducing curriculum and classroom-hour pressures.
      That is a separate context discussed in dictionaries and educational accounts.
      It does not justify judging a whole generation or deriving dojo policy from
      school reform. Here, the ordinary meaning matters: still having room to act.<sup><a href="#yutori-source-3">[3]</a></sup>
    </p>

    <h2>Where did this repetition run out of room?</h2>
    <p>
      Return to the pair. Telling them to relax is easy. It is less useful if they
      do not know what needs changing. Is the pace too high? Do both understand
      the sequence? Are they positioned so that their partner can receive the movement
      safely? Those are questions about an exercise, not judgements of character.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Time.</strong> A margin may mean working at a pace where both partners
      can recognise the task and respond to a correction. It is not a blanket
      instruction to enter earlier. Earlier is not always better: timing depends
      on the form, distance and teacher's instructions. If every repetition needs
      rescuing through haste, revisit those agreements.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Space.</strong> The position must suit the exercise. Distance,
      called <em>maai</em> in budō, is not simply an empty gap between people.
      Too far away can be as unhelpful as too close. The pair needs room for the
      agreed movement and for
      <a href="/en/blog/ukemi-falling-safely-keeping-structure-returning-to-action.html">ukemi</a>:
      receiving the technique and falling safely. The aim is an appropriate
      distance, not the largest possible one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Attention.</strong> Room to adjust means not being occupied solely
      with finishing the technique. You still notice your partner's movement
      and whether the agreed form remains possible. You do not force a plan
      that no longer fits. Reducing the number of elements in an exercise
      may help more than adding several new instructions at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      Breathing and tension are worth noticing, but they are not a simple gauge
      of readiness. Raised shoulders may suggest difficulty; they do not prove
      laziness, cowardice or lack of skill. If you begin holding your breath
      and losing control during a faster sequence, tell the teacher and slow down
      or stop. Do not force deeper breathing to sustain an excessive pace.
    </p>

    <h2>The margin belongs to both partners</h2>
    <p>
      Aikido is partner practice. Nage feeling comfortable does not mean uke
      has equally suitable conditions. An experienced person may easily perform
      a movement their partner cannot yet receive safely. One person's spare
      capacity is not enough for good practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      The instructor sets the form and intensity to suit both partners.
      Either person may slow down or stop. We do not test a partner's margin
      by unexpectedly speeding up or attacking harder. Our article on
      <a href="/en/blog/uke-and-nage-a-learning-relationship.html">uke and nage</a>
      explains that agreement in more detail.
    </p>
    <p>
      A margin is not a safety guarantee. Skill, fatigue, health, surroundings
      and your partner's response still require attention. It may, however,
      create conditions where you do not have to fight the pace to make a correction.
      In the opening scene, a useful question is whether both people can pause,
      listen to an instruction and resume without pulling or rushing.
    </p>

    <h2>High standards can coexist with a quiet pace</h2>
    <p>
      Slow practice can be demanding. You must maintain posture, attend to contact
      and avoid hiding mistakes behind momentum. Speed is not the only way to
      increase difficulty. A teacher can keep the pace quiet while asking for
      more precise work on one element.
    </p>
    <p>
      Conditions can change as training develops. The aim is not to remain forever
      with the easiest task, but to know why we are increasing difficulty and how
      to respond when it exceeds the pair's abilities. Doing less today may be
      appropriate when tired, returning after a break or rebuilding practice.
      Rest and recovery are not the opposite of yutori.
    </p>
    <p>
      Discipline alone does not create every kind of reserve. Health, responsibilities
      and circumstances differ. Someone arriving after a difficult day does not
      need a lecture about managing themselves better. They need an honest
      conversation about how they can work today. A training plan can be adapted;
      it is not a measure of a person's worth.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our article on <a href="/en/blog/kakugo-enter-whole-or-not-at-all.html">kakugo</a>
      distinguishes resolve from intensity. Yutori raises a different question:
      do the conditions leave room to carry out that decision responsibly?
      For this teaching purpose, the concepts complement one another.
      Resolve does not automatically create spare time, energy or space.
    </p>

    <h2>One delay in an ordinary day</h2>
    <p>
      Consider a day when every appointment ends just as the next one begins.
      The first conversation runs a few minutes late. Soon you are watching the clock
      instead of listening and trying to recover time by hurrying everything else.
      That is not proof of weak character. The plan may simply have left no room
      for something it could not predict.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you control the schedule, leaving a gap or removing one task may change
      the quality of the rest of the day. If you do not have that freedom,
      the necessary conversation may concern workload, shared responsibilities
      or priorities. A margin is not always created by inner calm. Sometimes
      it requires changes no individual can make alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is an analogy with training, not a solution for every circumstance.
      Not everyone can save money, rearrange obligations or get more sleep at will.
      The useful question is how to recognise a limit and find an available change,
      rather than blame yourself for lacking an ideal reserve.
    </p>
    <p>
      At your next practice, choose one element to examine with the teacher:
      pace, position or the number of things you are trying to do at once.
      See whether changing it lets both partners notice and respond to a correction.
      <strong>Yutori need not look impressive. It matters that there is still room
      for the next sensible movement.</strong>
    </p>

    <h2>Sources and interpretation</h2>
    <p>
      The partner scene and calendar example are the author's illustrations.
      Applying yutori to practice is a teaching proposal, not a definition for
      every Aikido school or a claim about injury rates. Dictionaries support
      the meanings of reserve and leeway; they do not establish the effectiveness
      of a particular exercise.
    </p>
